# Endings: checked in this order on arrival day; the last one has no conditions.
# Each ending is one carrier clause (the fleet's hail vote) times one arrival clause
# (the storm vote: held the plan, waited it out in orbit, or no storm). Every ending
# closes on the first crate. `title` is the heading; see STORY_BIBLE.md.

card end_lights
type ending
cat ending
title Lights On
when flag signal_answered
when flag arrive_hold
text In every cockpit, at four hundred metres in the brown, the cameras give up. Low on the bearing Cargo 2's radio gives, the dust starts to glow white from underneath. The review board, sitting at last, cleared one more command, and the one who answered sent it: lights. Cargo 2 is spending its saved battery on every floodlight it has. One by one seven ships steer for the glow and come down on the flat, a safe three kilometres short. The first crate out of Cargo 2's hold says, in Sharpie: "You'll thank me." To Starbase: "Landed. All eight."

card end_through
type ending
cat ending
title Through the Dust
when flag arrive_hold
text Holding the plan means flying the last kilometres half blind: radar for height, the last orbiter fix for place, and a knock on the old channel every eleven seconds to cross-check, still asking. Six ships land three kilometres from Cargo 2. The seventh is the one the gust caught: upright on the slope, a leg strut folded under it. It will not fly home. Two sols later the first suit out, the one who lost the vote to answer, climbs the rim and answers from two hundred metres. HELLO RX · OK. To Starbase: "Landed. Seven down, six to fly. Found the eighth."

card end_long_orbit
type ending
cat ending
title The Long Orbit
when flag signal_answered
when flag arrive_wait
text Aerocapture, as voted: two passes through the heat instead of one. On the second, a tile on one hinge line runs hotter than the brief allowed, and holds. Then the long orbit. Week three, night pass: half rations on seven galley tables, Cargo 2's manifest, cleared by the board at last, coming down a line a pass, read aloud in turn. Tonight's line is a spare drill bit, and seven hungry people cheer a drill bit. A week later the storm clears, seven ships land, and the rover drives out to a lamp that has burned steady for weeks. In its hold the first crate says, in Sharpie: "You'll thank me." To Starbase: "Landed. All eight."

card end_patience
type ending
cat ending
title Patience
when flag arrive_wait
text Aerocapture, as voted. The ground's hold stands, no first handshake without a suit beside it, and from the long orbit the site rises for minutes a day, low behind the crater rim. Week two: ration bars cut in half on seven tables, and the knock later every night, and every night back. The night before the fleet lands, it isn't. The first suit out, who lost the vote to answer, reaches it at dawn: strobe dark, frost on the wing. A battery pack on the service port, and it wakes, slow, and knocks once. The answer goes in by hand, from two metres, inside the tick. HELLO RX · OK. To Starbase: "Landed. All seven. Found the eighth."

card end_friend
type ending
cat ending
title Old Friend
when flag signal_answered
text Seven ships come down in clear air, thirty kilometres from a light that has held steady since the window. On the second sol the one who packed the bag drives out with the rover's radio on the old channel. As it closes, Cargo 2 runs the pad crew's arrival routine, written for a truck from the hangar: floods, strobe, hatch lamp. The driver hooks the rover's last spare battery to its service port and drives home slow. By morning Cargo 2 is talking through the fleet to Earth: two years of logs, then its manifest, drill spares first. In its hold the first crate says, in Sharpie: "You'll thank me." To Starbase: "Landed. All eight."

card end_close
type ending
cat ending
title Close Enough
text Seven ships land in clear air. On the first sol Cargo 3's ice drill snaps its only bit, and the fleet's water margin starts going down a day at a time; the spare is on a written-off manifest, thirty kilometres off. On the third sol the one who packed a bag for this drive takes the rover out, and answers the next knock from two hundred metres. The screen shows one line: HELLO RX · OK. Then the strobe goes out, and stays out: two years of asking, and the battery had one answer left. The bit is where the manifest said. To Starbase: "Landed. All seven. Found the eighth, in time."
